WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.409 --> 00:00:04.550
While Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse is
totally suitable for children, it has some

00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:09.139
truly great moments that older viewers can
appreciate — moments that likely swung right

00:00:09.140 --> 00:00:09.980
over the kiddies' heads.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:14.200
These are some of the things that only adults
will notice in Into the Spider-Verse.

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:19.240
Into the Spider-Verse feels like a movie that
is fully aware of the fact that it's inspired

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:20.000
by a comic book.

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:24.720
The film begins with an introduction from
Peter Parker… well, a Peter Parker, as we

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.800
later meet a couple different versions of
him.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.580
If you pick up on the context clues, you can
determine that the Peter from the beginning

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:36.240
of the film is actually Tobey Maguire's Spider-Man
from the original Sam Raimi trilogy.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:41.040
The film smartly displays animated versions
of scenes from those movies, including Peter

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:47.100
stopping the runaway train in 2004's Spider-Man
2 and even his weird dance from 2007's Spider-Man 3.

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:52.020
Rumor has it that Sony actually even considered
casting Maguire to voice this version of Parker

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:54.900
before ultimately settling on Chris Pine.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:59.640
Beyond that, Peter goes on to brag that he's
been featured on a slew of various merchandise,

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.379
including a comic book, a cereal …

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:03.140
"And a so-so popsicle.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:04.070
I mean, I've looked worse."

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:05.570
Doc Ock tentacle slams him into wall.

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:09.770
He also mentions that he has "an excellent
theme song," which fans of the early animated

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:11.840
cartoon shows can certainly vouch for.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:17.200
While this surplus of Spidey stuff is sure
to give older fans fits of nostalgia, you

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:20.940
have to wonder how much of it went unnoticed
by the younger generation.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:25.050
After Miles is bit by the radioactive spider,
his body starts to change.

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:29.220
He wakes up the next morning and notices that
his pants are now too short because he grew

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:30.220
in height.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:33.400
Seeking an explanation, he wonders if he's
possibly going through puberty.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:37.890
Later, he finds himself noticeably sweating
while trying to pull off Uncle Aaron's "shoulder

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:40.050
touch" pick-up move with Gwen.

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:43.640
When she asks him straight-up why he's so
sweaty, he tells her:

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:46.450
"It's a puberty thing."

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:50.810
He continues to stick with this story throughout
the scene, even when his newly acquired spider-grip

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:55.310
gets stuck in Gwen's hair, causing her to
exclaim, "I don't think you know what puberty

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:55.880
is!"

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:59.440
The scene is humorous to adults because they
know that obviously puberty has nothing to

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:03.600
do with gaining spider powers — and everything
to do with the awkward teenager-ness that

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.900
inspired Miles to try out Uncle Aaron's move
in the first place.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:12.520
However, for a kid who hasn't gone through
puberty, they might not fully grasp the outlandish

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:13.830
nature of Miles' assessment.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:16.880
If only it truly did grant you superpowers!

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:18.560
Better than acne and B.O., right?

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:23.120
You don't have to be Sherlock Holmes to immediately
figure out that in Into the Spider-Verse,

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:26.740
Miles' Uncle Aaron was going to play an important
role in the young hero's story.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:31.330
Fans of the comics knew that he was actually
the Prowler, but even if you didn't know that

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:35.870
going in, it became pretty obvious early on
that good ol' Uncle Aaron had a few tricks

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:36.870
up his sleeve.

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:40.630
However, when you consider that this is a
Spider-Man movie, the term "uncle" should

00:02:40.630 --> 00:02:41.140
be a red flag.

00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:45.940
Everyone knows that it took the death of his
Uncle Ben for Peter Parker to realize that

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:49.220
his actions have consequences, and that he
can't always save everyone.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:50.640
"Uncle Ben?"

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:55.400
"Peter."

00:02:57.360 --> 00:02:58.320
"I'm here, Uncle Ben."

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:03.100
For young Miles, it's the death of his Uncle
Aaron.

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:04.400
The parallel between the two characters is
a brilliant facet of the film that is both

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:05.640
mature and thought-provoking.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:07.620
“You're on your way.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:11.700
Just keep going.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:15.320
Just keep going."

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:19.770
Eagle-eyed audience members most likely picked
up on a hearty portion of the various Easter

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:22.360
eggs featured in Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:26.590
In one of the film's many blink-and-you-miss-it
moments, viewers get a peek at Miles' dad's

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:27.720
phone contacts.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:32.280
Although Jefferson Davis is trying to call
his brother, Aaron, you can't help but notice

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:35.760
another, more prominent name on the list:
Steve Ditko.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:39.840
Marvel fanboys will recognize that as a nod
to the late comic book artist who, along with

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.900
Stan Lee, co-created Spider-Man and Doctor
Strange.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:46.820
However, the subtle name-dropping doesn't
stop there.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:49.910
In another scene, Miles takes out his phone
to make a call, and the audience yet again

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:51.260
gets a glimpse at his phonebook.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:56.160
The most obvious attention-grabbers are the
names B. Bendis — alluding to Brian Michael

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:57.860
Bendis — and Sara Pichelli.

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:03.180
This duo co-wrote Miles Morales' first series,
2011's Ultimate Comics Spider-Man.

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:08.420
You probably only noticed these subtle details
if names like Ditko, Bendis, and Pichelli

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:13.770
meant something to you, and, for many now-adult
comic readers in the '90s and early 2000s,

00:04:13.770 --> 00:04:14.560
that was the case.

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:19.400
When it's finally his turn to return to his
own universe, John Mulaney's Peter Porker,

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:24.390
a.k.a. Spider-Ham, gives Miles a touching
farewell, humorously gifting him a massive

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:25.080
wooden hammer…

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:27.700
"I want you to have this.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.200
It'll fit in your pocket."

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:30.900
… before making the jump home.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.860
Before he ascends, he sadly declares…

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:36.140
"That's all, folks."

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:39.860
… prompting Peter B. Parker to ask if he
was allowed to say that, quote, "legally?"

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:45.000
The line is obviously a reference to the Looney
Tunes character Porky Pig, owned by Warner

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.600
Bros. — who you can assume has a trademark
on the infamous phrase.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:52.640
By questioning the legality of using the line,
Peter breaks the fourth wall to call the audience's

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:56.340
attention to the cartoon pig that isn't Porky
saying his famous line.

00:04:56.920 --> 00:05:00.780
It's a meta nod to a competitor's classic
character, but its humor is probably lost

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:04.880
on children, since, well… they probably
aren't all too familiar with the concept of

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:05.800
copyright infringement.

00:05:06.850 --> 00:05:11.080
Into the Spider-Verse really starts to gain
traction when Peter B. Parker enters the fold

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:14.720
— a chubbier, more depressed Spider-Man
than the one from Miles' universe.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:19.560
During his introductory scene, the audience
learns that this more jaded, broken-down version

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:23.580
of Peter ended things with the Mary Jane from
his universe because, well…

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:28.240
"She wanted kids, and… it scared me."

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:33.600
Although it's presented in a humorous light,
this arc of Peter learning that he can, in

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:37.240
fact, become a good parent is present throughout
the movie, for those clever enough to pick

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:37.760
up on it.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:42.530
It doesn't take a rocket scientist to realize
that the relationship between Peter and Miles

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:44.560
is not unlike that of a father and son.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:49.780
Miles, the in-over-his-head apprentice, seeks
guidance from Peter, the weary master who

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:52.460
is so experienced that he almost can't help
himself from giving advice.

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:58.260
By the end of the movie, Peter gets to witness
firsthand the effect his presence had on Miles'

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:00.980
heroic growth, and it's a feeling that gives
him immense pride.

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:03.460
In the climactic final battle, Peter says…

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:04.700
"I love you.

00:06:04.700 --> 00:06:06.180
I'm so proud of you.

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:07.200
Do I want kids?"

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:11.200
Most parents in the audience surely understood
Peter's weighty realization.

